  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Common Roadside-Skipper is a small butterfly belonging to the Skippers family, characterized by its modest size and quick, erratic flight pattern. Despite its plain appearance, it is one of the most abundant skippers found throughout New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Wing Pattern",
      "description": "Dark brown wings with faint spots and a checkered fringe pattern along wing edges",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Size",
      "description": "Very small butterfly with a wingspan of only 0.9-1.2 inches, making it one of the tiniest skippers",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Behavior",
      "description": "Flies close to the ground in a rapid, darting motion, staying near roadside vegetation",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found in open, disturbed areas such as roadsides, field edges, and grassy clearings with low vegetation, particularly where native grasses grow.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The Common Roadside-Skipper gets its name from its preference for hanging around roadsides and its characteristic habit of resting on low plants with its wings folded in a tent-like position.",
